{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["And the reason of <b>He will destroy them and He will subdue them</b> — it is like saying \"He will subdue them and He will destroy them.\" <b>יַכְנִיעֵם</b> (He will subdue them) is an expression of lowliness, as in \"because Ahab humbled himself\" (1 Kings 21:29), or \"then their uncircumcised heart shall be humbled\" (Leviticus 26:41), and so all of them. And the reason is that those nations were exceedingly afraid of Israel, and with enraged heart and lowly spirit and slackened hands they would go out against them to battle, as it says: \"And it came to pass, when all the kings of the Amorites heard, etc.\" and \"all the kings of the Canaanites, etc.\" \"and their heart melted, and there was no more spirit in them because of the children of Israel\" (Joshua 5:1). And it is possible that <b>He will destroy them</b> alludes to great and mighty nations, and <b>He will subdue them</b> to the children of the Anakim, for despite all their might and their stature their heart was subdued more than the rest of the people, and they did not go out to battle at all, but rather hid in the mountains and in the fortified cities, as it says: \"And Joshua came at that time, and cut off the Anakim from the hill-country, from Hebron, from Debir, from Anab, and from all the hill-country of Judah, and from all the hill-country of Israel; with their cities Joshua devoted them\" (Joshua 11:21) — that is, in their cities. And likewise: \"And Judah went against the Canaanite who dwelt in Hebron, and they struck Sheshai and Ahiman and Talmai\" (Judges 1:10)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:47:04.017809604Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}